analysis.originalLogic:
In the SSA-local copy-class computation used by CopyProp, `compute_copy_classes` walks copy assignments. For a copy whose right-hand side reaches a representative `head` (`let head = copies[rhs];`), it decides whether to merge `local` into `head`'s copy class. The original guard consulted `borrowed_classes` and skipped unification only when BOTH endpoints were borrowed:

```rust
// Do not unify two borrowed locals.
if borrowed_classes.contains(local) && borrowed_classes.contains(head) {
    continue;
}
```

Thus a copy `local = copy head` was still unified when exactly one of `local`/`head` had its address taken.

analysis.incorrectTransformation:
When only one endpoint of a copy edge was in `borrowed_classes`, the old `&&` guard allowed the two locals to be placed in the same copy class. CopyProp could then eliminate the copy (`_2 = copy _1;` disappearing) and rewrite reads of the borrowed local into reads of the other local (for example `opaque::<T>(copy _2)` becoming `opaque::<T>(copy _1)`, or in the loop case deleting `_10 = copy _6; _1 = move _10;` after treating `_1` and `_6` as interchangeable). That rewrite is not valid when the borrowed local is written after its address is taken and the borrow is read again later: the unified storage can be overwritten while the reference still points at it. The corrected invariant is that unification is forbidden if EITHER endpoint is borrowed:

```rust
// Do not unify borrowed locals.
if borrowed_classes.contains(local) || borrowed_classes.contains(head) {
    continue;
}
```

so the copy into the borrowed local remains (`_1 = copy _6`) instead of merging the two storages.
